	\begin{abstract}
		Urban traffic management faces challenges of congestion, delays, and safety risks due to rapid urbanization and increasing vehicle density. Traditional fixed or density-based traffic signal systems cannot adapt efficiently to dynamic traffic patterns and do not provide safety guarantees. Adaptive systems, such as RHODES, improve real-time control but rely on empirical models without mathematical proof of safety. This research integrates formal verification methods with real-time adaptive traffic control, ensuring provably safe, error-free signal operation while maintaining optimized traffic flow.
	\end{abstract}
	
	\section*{Keywords}
	Adaptive Traffic Control, RHODES, Formal Methods, Model Checking, Urban Mobility, Safety Assurance
	
	\section{Introduction}
	Urban traffic management has become increasingly challenging due to rapid urbanization, growing vehicle density, and limited infrastructure. Congestion leads to delays, higher accident rates, and increased fuel consumption. Emergency vehicles such as ambulances and fire trucks often face critical delays in high-density traffic.
	
	Traditional traffic control systems, including fixed-time and simple density-based signals, are limited in adapting to dynamic traffic conditions. Early adaptive systems used microcontrollers and IR sensors to adjust signal timings. While they improved traffic flow and allowed emergency prioritization, they lacked formal verification to ensure safety under all circumstances.
	
	Modern adaptive systems like RHODES employ predictive, hierarchical algorithms using rolling time horizons and platoon-based modeling. IoT-enabled traffic management further improves capabilities with sensors, cameras, V2V/V2I communication, and RFID tracking. These systems dynamically adjust signals and provide real-time information. However, they rely on empirical data and software logic without formal proof of safety.
	
	This research proposes integrating formal verification methods with adaptive traffic control to ensure that no two conflicting signals are active simultaneously, emergency vehicles receive priority, and traffic flow is optimized under all conditions.
	
	\section{Main Body}
	
	\subsection{Early and Modern Adaptive Traffic Control Systems}
	
	\textbf{Early Systems:}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Microcontroller-based systems with IR sensors to detect traffic density.
		\item Dynamically adjusted green-light durations.
		\item Emergency vehicle priority via manual overrides.
		\item Limitation: Lacked formal verification, allowing unsafe signal conflicts.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\textbf{Modern Adaptive IoT-Based Systems:}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Integration of V2V and V2I communication enables real-time data sharing.
		\item IoT sensors, cameras, and RFID tracking improve detection and emergency prioritization.
		\item Intelligent algorithms adjust signals dynamically and predict congestion.
		\item Limitation: Susceptible to sensor failure, network delay, and software bugs; no formal proof ensures safety.
