	\subsection{RHODES Adaptive Control System}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Hierarchical and predictive approach with rolling time horizons and platoon-based modeling.
		\item Reduces waiting times, improves emergency response, and shows proven improvements in simulations.
		\item Limitation: Relies on empirical predictive models; no formal safety proof.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\section{Challenges and Future Perspectives}
	
	\subsection{Heterogeneous Data Integration}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item TMS must integrate data from multiple sources: sensors, cameras, V2V/V2I devices, IoT platforms.
		\item Challenges: lack of standardization, managing numerous IoT devices, securing private data.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\subsection{Data Management and Big Data Issues}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Handle large volumes of heterogeneous and asynchronous data.
		\item Challenges: standardization, data correlation, aggregation, and real-time decision-making.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\subsection{Future Perspectives}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Scalability to multi-intersection and city-wide networks.
		\item Advanced sensing: LIDAR, cameras, video analytics, machine learning.
		\item Resilience to sensor, network, and software failures.
		\item Energy efficiency and integration with urban traffic policies.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\section{Conclusion}
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Adaptive traffic and IoT-based systems improve efficiency and emergency vehicle prioritization.
		\item Empirical optimization alone cannot guarantee safety.
		\item Formal verification ensures provably safe operations within stated assumptions:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Mutual exclusion of green lights.
			\item Emergency vehicle prioritization.
			\item Optimized traffic flow under all conditions.
		\end{itemize}
		\item Verified adaptive systems provide a reliable foundation for future urban traffic management.
	\end{itemize}
	
